<h2>The application and its history</h2>
<p>Karan Rai, in custody since 17 April 2023 in FIR No. 53/2023 of Police Station Tughlak Road, applied for regular bail under Section 483 of the BNSS. The procedural history mattered: the Sessions Court had dismissed bail pleas on 1 October 2024 and again on 1 June 2026, and an earlier High Court application had been withdrawn in February 2025 with liberty to reapply. The prosecution’s case attributes to him a planning role in the group attack on the deceased, together with overt acts — throwing a brick and restraining the victim while others stabbed him.</p>

<h2>The defence attack on the dying declaration</h2>
<p>The defence trained its fire on the alleged oral dying declaration. The deceased, it argued, was reportedly semi-conscious; the names do not figure in the FIR or the hospital records; the auto-rickshaw driver who carried the victim was never examined; the clothing of the witnesses was not seized; no stab injury or weapon is attributed to the applicant; and the CCTV footage does not show the assault. Added to this were the familiar long-custody factors: a trial with 58 cited witnesses moving slowly, family ties, ongoing education, and compliance with earlier interim bail.</p>

<h2>Why the Court refused</h2>
<p>Justice Sanjeev Narula declined to atomise the material. The circumstances, the judgment holds, <strong>"must be considered together, rather than in isolation."</strong> Three strands anchored the refusal:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Two direct witnesses</h4><p>PW-1 and PW-2 deposed that the dying victim named the applicant, and PW-1 identified him in CCTV footage. The Court said it "cannot disregard the testimony of two witnesses who specifically claim to have heard the deceased identify the Applicant."</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Admissibility vs. weight</h4><p>An oral dying declaration "is not rendered inadmissible merely because it was not recorded by a doctor." The defence objections go to weight and credibility — questions for the trial court after full evidence.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>An active attributed role</h4><p>The alleged planning role and overt acts supplied a prima facie basis of involvement under Sections 34 and 120-B; whether common intention or conspiracy is ultimately proved remains open at trial.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The Court expressly acknowledged that the seriousness of the offence cannot by itself justify continued detention, and it weighed the custody period and trial pace in the applicant’s favour. But with material witnesses on motive and conspiracy yet to be examined — and with specific, role-attributing testimony already on record — the balance went against release. Notably, the Court drew no adverse inference from an earlier FIR under Section 324 IPC, confining itself to the material in the present case. The refusal came with a counterweight: a direction to the trial court to expedite the recording of prosecution evidence, especially the remaining material witnesses, and to avoid unnecessary adjournments.</p>

<h2>The pair of judgments as a bail-stage map</h2>
<p>Read with the companion order granting bail to co-accused Sourab Rawat and Ankit Kumar in the same FIR — covered separately on this site — the case supplies a rare controlled comparison. Same incident, same FIR, same judge, same day; opposite outcomes. The variable was not the charge, the custody period or the trial’s pace, all of which were common. It was the existence of witness testimony attributing a specific role to the specific applicant. Where that material existed, bail was refused and the trial expedited; where it did not, conditions were judged sufficient and bail followed. Parity arguments in multi-accused cases will succeed or fail on precisely this distinction.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>For the defence, a refusal of this kind is not the end of the road: the expedition direction itself becomes the next application’s foundation. If the material witnesses are examined and the attributed role does not survive cross-examination — or if the trial stalls despite the direction — a fresh application on changed circumstances stands on demonstrably stronger ground.</p>
</div>
